'''textorial''' (teks-tō′ri-ạl), ''a.'' [< L. ''textorius'', of or pertaining to weaving, < ''textor'', a weaver, < ''texere'', weave: see ''text''.] Of or pertaining to weaving. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|From the cultivation of the ''textorial'' arts among the orientals came Darius's wonderful cloth.
{{right|''T. Warton'', Hist. Eng. Poetry, iii. 178.}}}}

'''Textor's map-projection.''' See ''projection.''

'''text-pen''' (tekst′pen), ''n.'' A kind of metallic pen used in engrossing.

'''textrine''' (teks′trin), ''a.'' [< L. ''textrinus'', of or pertaining to weaving, contr. from *''textorinus'', < ''textor'', a weaver: see ''textorial''.] Of or pertaining to weaving or construction; textorial. ''Derham'', Physico-Theol., viii. 6. [Rare.]

'''textual''' (teks′t̮ū-ạl), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< ME. ''textuel'', < OF. (and F.) ''textuel'' = Sp. Pg. ''textual'' = It. ''testuale'', < L. as if *''textualis'', < ''textus'', text: see ''text''.] '''I.''' ''a.'' 1. Of, pertaining to, or contained in the text: as, ''textual'' criticism; ''textual'' errors.

{{smaller block|They seek . . . to rout and disarray the wise and well-couched order of St. Paul's own words, using a certain ''textual'' riot to chop off the hands of the word presbytery.
{{right|''Milton'', On Def. of Humb. Remonst., § 5.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Textual'' inaccuracy is a grave fault in the new edition of the old poets. {{right|''Lowell'', Study Windows, p. 301.}}}}

2†. Based on texts.

{{smaller block|Here shall your majestie find . . . speculation interchanged with experience, positive theology with polemical, ''textual'' with discursorie. {{right|''Bp. Hall'', Works, Ded.}}}}

