5. In ''biol.'', a tissue; the character or mode of formation of tissues.—6. In the ''fine arts'', the surface quality of animate or inanimate objects, natural or artificial, which expresses to the eye the disposition and arrangement of their component tissues.—'''Cavernous texture.''' {{smaller|See ''cavernous''.—'''Texture of rocks''', the mode of aggregation of the mineral substances of which rocks are composed. It relates to the arrangement of their parts viewed on a smaller scale than that of their structure. The texture of rocks may be compact, earthy, granular, scaly, slaty, etc. See ''structure''.}}

'''texture''' (teks′t̮ūr), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''textured'', ppr. ''texturing''. [< ''texture'', ''n.''] To form a texture of or with; interweave. [Rare.]

'''textureless''' (teks′t̮ūr-les), ''a.'' [< ''texture'' + ''-less''.] Having no discernible structure; amorphous: as, a ''textureless'' membrane.

'''textury†''' (teks′t̮ū-ri), ''a.'' [< ''texture'' + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] Same as ''texture'', 1.

'''textus''' (teks′tus), ''n.'' [< L. ''textus'', text: see ''text''.] 1. The text of any book, especially of the Bible or of a part of it: as, the ''Textus'' Receptus (see phrase below).—2†. A book containing the liturgical gospels.

{{smaller block|The book of the gospels, or ''textus'', had, in general, a binding of solid gold, studded with gems, and especially pearls, and was used for being kissed; the other, the gospel-book, which served for reading out of, was often as richly adorned. {{right|''Rock'', Church of our Fathers, III. ii. 192.}}}}

